  "question": {
    "text": "Electron gain enthalpy with negative sign of fluorine is less than that of chlorine due to :",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "Br3vh",
      "text": "\nHigh ionization enthalpy of fluorine\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "okgIQ",
      "text": "\nSmaller size of chlorine atom\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "aUc1s",
      "text": "\nSmaller size of fluorine atom\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "sKonS",
      "text": "\nBigger size of $2 p$ orbital of fluorine",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "The electron gain enthalpy order for halogens is\n$\\mathrm{Cl}>\\mathrm{F}>\\mathrm{Br}>\\mathrm{I}$\nDue to small size of fluorine the extra electron to be added feels more electronelectron repulsion. Therefore fluorine has less value for electron affinity than chlorine.",
